{"status":"ready","summary":"This bundles the fallback block reuse cache with the large perfect hash aggregate (HA). The tree builds. On the full visible bench, q13 is 25-27% faster in every run and the geomean improved by 2.8% and 4.8% in the last two runs. No template was consistently slower.

(a) Block cache, in BlockAllocator (block_allocator.cpp/.hpp, plus buffer_pool.cpp calling SetFallbackCacheLimit). It only applies when the block pool is not in use. Freed 256KB blocks go onto a mutex-protected LIFO stack and are handed back on the next block-size allocation. The cache holds at most min(256MB, memory_limit/32). It is emptied in ThreadFlush, FlushAll, Resize (when the pool is turned on), the limit setter and ~BlockAllocator.

(b) Large perfect HA (plan_aggregate.cpp, physical_perfecthash_aggregate.cpp/.hpp, perfect_aggregate_hashtable.cpp/.hpp). The planner allows up to 22 bits when perfect_ht_threshold >= 12, under three guards: estimated input >= slots/2; slots*(state+1)*(threads+1) <= max_memory/8; and no DELIM_SCAN in the input subtree. Large mode adds:
- per-slot state initialization only when a slot is first used;
- combine done in 64K-slot stripes, each with its own lock;
- a parallel source that scans 64K-slot blocks;
- SourceOrder NO_ORDER.
The non-large path is unchanged (I made its SourceOrder INSERTION_ORDER again, as before). Large tables are allocated with new[], the same as the non-large path.

Measurements (best -> mine):
- q13: 510-518 -> 375-386 ms (-24.5 to -27.4%) in all 8 bench runs.
- Full bench, 256MB cache: geomean -4.8% and -2.8%. q18 -3.2% / -5.6%. q16 -3.2% / +5.1%. q03 -11.2% / +12.2% (noise). A final subset run gave q16 +2.4%, q18 -1.7%.
- Full bench, 64MB cache (the planned size): q16 +4.8% / +6.7% / +5.7%, so I raised the cap.
- Full bench, no cache: q18 +8.6%, q16 +11.3%, q14 +7.7%. This reproduces the slowdown that got the large perfect HA retired before.","generality":"The large perfect HA helps GROUP BY on one or more integer keys whose stats give a range of up to 2^22 slots with many rows per group, typically dense foreign keys. In those cases it replaces a radix hash aggregate. It does not apply to:
- inputs estimated sparser than slots/2;
- tables whose estimated memory exceeds max_memory/8;
- plans with a DELIM_SCAN under the aggregate;
- DISTINCT aggregates, or aggregates that have no combine function.

The block cache helps any workload that keeps allocating and freeing 256KB buffer blocks (hash join builds, aggregates, sorts) while the block pool is not in use. That is the default (block_allocator_memory=0). It holds at most min(256MB, memory_limit/32) of extra memory and is emptied whenever memory is flushed.","notes":"- The slowdown in q14/q16/q18 after q13 in the full bench is not caused by memory the large perfect HA tables leave behind. Two tests ruled that out. Calling Allocator::FlushAll when the large HA's global state is destroyed did not fix the later templates, and it cut q13's gain to -12% because the flush runs inside the query. Allocating the tables with glibc new[] (mmap) instead of jemalloc also left the slowdowns in place. I kept new[] anyway because it matches the non-large path.
- Likely cause: in the baseline, q13's radix aggregate allocates and frees many 256KB blocks, which leaves jemalloc warm for the queries that follow. With the large perfect HA that no longer happens, and the block cache has to stand in for it. The cache size mattered: 0MB gave q16 +11%; 64MB gave q16 +5 to +7%; 256MB gave q16 and q18 neutral or faster.
- Switching the cache from the FIFO moodycamel queue to a LIFO stack made no measurable difference at 64MB. I kept the stack because the code is simpler.
- I did not run the planned single-process sql sequence check, because the full bench gave the same information directly.
- Known risk, measured earlier: sparse inputs with overestimated cardinality ran +60% slower on a 1.8M-row, 2M-slot case. Bench noise is about ±10% on q03, q04 and q16."}

structured_output.generality:
The large perfect HA helps GROUP BY on one or more integer keys whose stats give a range of up to 2^22 slots with many rows per group, typically dense foreign keys. In those cases it replaces a radix hash aggregate. It does not apply to:
- inputs estimated sparser than slots/2;
- tables whose estimated memory exceeds max_memory/8;
- plans with a DELIM_SCAN under the aggregate;
- DISTINCT aggregates, or aggregates that have no combine function.

The block cache helps any workload that keeps allocating and freeing 256KB buffer blocks (hash join builds, aggregates, sorts) while the block pool is not in use. That is the default (block_allocator_memory=0). It holds at most min(256MB, memory_limit/32) of extra memory and is emptied whenever memory is flushed.
